UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 7, Lesson 4: Aural skills and listening', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Tempo is the speed of the beat, from slow to fast, and dynamics are the loudness, from soft to loud. They are expressive choices: the same body pattern can feel cautious, urgent, heavy, or gentle when you change only speed or only volume. A choice needs a reason. You name the tempo, name the dynamics, and say what mood that pair suggests to you in this example. Fast is not locked to happy, and loud is not locked to angry. The mood comes from the combination and from the picture you are making. You can perform every example with pats, stamps, or a hum, then write the reason in pencil.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same arts idea to a new artwork or performance, then compare two choices for clarity of effect.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nStart with eight quiet finger clicks that gradually get faster, then switch to four slow loud stamps. What happens to the mood, and which choice, tempo or dynamics, does each half use?\n\nFEEDBACK\nA strong answer separates speed from volume and ties each half to a mood with a because. The clicks stay soft while the tempo quickens, so the mood can tighten or flutter without becoming a shout. The stamps are both slower and louder, so the mood can turn heavy or final. Saying only \"it got more exciting\" never names the choice that caused the change.\n\nWATCH FOR\nFast music is always happy and loud music is always angry, so each speed and volume has one fixed mood. That fails because the same fast tempo can feel playful in a game or nervous in a chase, and a loud sound can feel proud, warning, or simply clear. A mood word without the tempo or dynamic that supports it is a guess. Match the word to the choice you actually performed.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nHum one comfortable pitch three ways: slow and soft, then fast and soft, then slow and loud. Keep the pitch the same so the only changes are tempo and dynamics. Write two mood sentences, each naming which choice changed and why the feeling shifted. This hum task is not the knee-pat pair and not the click-then-stamp practice.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one artistic choice you changed and why. Movement or instrument work is optional, adult-supervised and can be described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Music","learning_intention":"Tempo is the speed of the beat, from slow to fast, and dynamics are the loudness, from soft to loud. They are expressive choices: the same body pattern can feel cautious, urgent, heavy, or gentle when you change only speed or only volume. A choice needs a reason. You name the tempo, name the dynamics, and say what mood that pair suggests to you in this example. Fast is not locked to happy, and loud is not locked to angry. The mood comes from the combination and from the picture you are making. You can perform every example with pats, stamps, or a hum, then write the reason in pencil.","lesson_number":4,"lesson_role":"Apply","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: scrap paper, crayons, safe classroom instruments or clear movement space with an adult nearby"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["MU4-LIS-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Hum one comfortable pitch three ways: slow and soft, then fast and soft, then slow and loud. Keep the pitch the same so the only changes are tempo and dynamics. Write two mood sentences, each naming which choice changed  Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using tempo on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: Fast music is always happy and loud music is always angry, so each speed and volume has one fixed mood.. Correction: A strong answer separates speed from volume and ties each half to a mood with a because.. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Fast music is always happy and loud music is always angry, so each speed and volume has one fixed mood. That fails because the same fast tempo can feel playful in a game or nervous in a chase, and a loud sound can feel proud, warning, or simply clear. A mood word without the tempo or dynamic that supports it is a guess. Match the word to the choice you actually performed. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating tempo on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with tempo, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about tempo with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for tempo. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for tempo. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A strong answer separates speed from volume and ties each half to a mood with a because","Fast music is always happy and loud music is always angry, so each speed and volume has one fixed mood","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named","Copy the example artwork or script and call that independent work"],"question":"Start with eight quiet finger clicks that gradually get faster, then switch to four slow loud stamps. What happens to the mood, and which choice, tempo or dynamics, does each half use?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for tempo; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A strong answer separates speed from volume and ties each half to a mood with a because","Careful use of dynamics with clear evidence","Fast music is always happy and loud music is always angry, so each speed and volume has one fixed mood","Tempo is the speed of the beat, from slow to fast, and dynamics are the loudness, from soft to loud"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with tempo?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named dynamics, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence","Fast music is always happy and loud music is always angry, so each speed and volume has one fixed mood","Name dynamics only, without linking it to tempo","Apply tempo to new material and name the dynamics used"],"question":"Which response best applies tempo with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"The stronger choice uses elements, techniques and audience effect appropriately for this lesson on tempo. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Copy another artwork’s sacred design without permission or guidance","Fast music is always happy and loud music is always angry, so each speed and volume has one fixed mood","A strong answer separates speed from volume and ties each half to a mood with a because","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named"],"question":"Which response best evaluates elements, techniques and audience effect when analysing tempo?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Fast music is always happy and loud music is always angry, so each speed and volume has one fixed mood","Name a specific element or technique and say what effect it creates for tempo","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named","Copy another artwork’s sacred design without permission or guidance"],"question":"Which evidence move best supports a claim about tempo?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea (Tempo is the speed of the beat, from slow to fast, and dynamics are the) with concrete detail. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Fast music is always happy and loud music is always angry, so each speed and volume has one fixed mood","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named","Copy another artwork’s sacred design without permission or guidance","Pat your knees four times at a slow tempo and a soft dynamic, as if you are crossing a dark room"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about tempo?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Accuracy means checking evidence and honouring limits or protocols. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete","Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster","Treat every claim as equally reliable without checking a source or cue","Name the artistic choice, its effect, and keep cultural protocols respectful"],"question":"Which action keeps elements, techniques and audience effect accurate for tempo?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the taught skill. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Fast music is always happy and loud music is always angry, so each speed and volume has one fixed mood","Name dynamics only, with no linked example","Hum one comfortable pitch three ways: slow and soft, then fast and soft, then slow and loud","Retype the worked example with no new example"],"question":"Which independent response best applies tempo to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about tempo with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating tempo on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using tempo on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for tempo. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with tempo, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: Fast music is always happy and loud music is always angry, so each speed and volume has one fixed mood.. Correction: A strong answer separates speed from volume and ties each half to a mood with a because.. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent artwork or performance notes and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s5-music-y9-w02-l2","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 4 alignment to Music 7–10 (2024) outcome MU4-LIS-01. Content-point mapping is pending. Not NESA verified. No copyrighted recordings are required; examples are original descriptions or student-made sounds.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S4","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise tempo.","Read the mission before you start making or performing.","Keep the audience effect of your choice in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not name an effect clearly."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the element or technique that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore elements, safety or respectful practice.","Prefer a named artistic reason over a guess that merely looks colourful."],"submit":["Confirm that your choice and reason are both present.","Proofread element names and safety notes.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact element, technique or cue before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key artistic choices.","Say what each choice does for the artwork or audience.","A final praise word alone is weaker than a named effect."],"teach2":["Compare a weak choice with a stronger one.","Change the element or technique, not only the decoration.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh subject or piece that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original work notes and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill on new material."]},"subject":"Music","success_criteria":["I can use tempo with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify dynamics and explain why it matters.","I can practise mood independently on a new prompt.","I can notice choice needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Tempo is the speed of the beat, from slow to fast, and dynamics are the loudness, from soft to loud.","options":["An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no artistic reason","Tempo is the speed of the beat, from slow to fast, and dynamics are the loudness, from soft to loud","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about tempo?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nPat your knees four times at a slow tempo and a soft dynamic, as if you are crossing a dark room. Pat the same four knee sounds again, now fast and loud, as if you need a friend at the other end of a yard. The rhythm did not change: four single pats. Tempo changed from slow to fast, and dynamics changed from soft to loud. The first version can feel careful. The second can feel urgent. Those moods are effects of the choices, not new notes. Write \"tempo\" and \"dynamics\" on two lines so you do not blend speed into volume.\n\nTRY FIRST\nStart with eight quiet finger clicks that gradually get faster, then switch to four slow loud stamps. What happens to the mood, and which choice, tempo or dynamics, does each half use?\n\nFEEDBACK\nA strong answer separates speed from volume and ties each half to a mood with a because. The clicks stay soft while the tempo quickens, so the mood can tighten or flutter without becoming a shout. The stamps are both slower and louder, so the mood can turn heavy or final. Saying only \"it got more exciting\" never names the choice that caused the change.","explain":"Tempo is the speed of the beat, from slow to fast, and dynamics are the loudness, from soft to loud. They are expressive choices: the same body pattern can feel cautious, urgent, heavy, or gentle when you change only speed or only volume. A choice needs a reason. You name the tempo, name the dynamics, and say what mood that pair suggests to you in this example. Fast is not locked to happy, and loud is not locked to angry. The mood comes from the combination and from the picture you are making. You can perform every example with pats, stamps, or a hum, then write the reason in pencil.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Fast music is always happy and loud music is always angry, so each speed and volume has one fixed mood. That fails because the same fast tempo can feel playful in a game or nervous in a chase, and a loud sound can feel proud, warning, or simply clear. A mood word without the tempo or dynamic that supports it is a guess. Match the word to the choice you actually performed. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"Two boxes separate tempo choices from dynamics choices.","caption":"Speed and loudness are different expressive choices.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Tempo</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">Slow beat or fast beat</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Dynamics</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">Soft sound or loud sound</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this example: Pat your knees four times at a slow tempo and a soft dynamic, as if you are crossing a dark room. Pat the same four knee Then write your own artistic reason.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.\nJuno: Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this example: Pat your knees four times at a slow tempo and a soft dynamic, as if you are crossing a dark room. Pat the same four knee Then write your own artistic reason."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no artistic reason","Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about tempo?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nPat your knees four times at a slow tempo and a soft dynamic, as if you are crossing a dark room. Pat the same four knee sounds again, now fast and loud, as if you need a friend at the other end of a yard. The rhythm did not change: four single pats. Tempo changed from slow to fast, and dynamics changed from soft to loud. The first version can feel careful. The second can feel urgent. Those moods are effects of the choices, not new notes. Write \"tempo\" and \"dynamics\" on two lines so you do not blend speed into volume. Look at each line, sound, movement or role choice that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key artistic choices from the example and say what each one does for the audience or artwork.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful response names the element or technique and its effect, not only “it looks nice”.","explain":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final praise word without naming a choice does not show arts thinking. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Show the artistic choice","visual":[{"alt":"Two boxes contrast a soft slow pattern with a loud fast pattern.","caption":"The same pats change mood when tempo and dynamics change.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Soft slow</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">Careful, quiet knee pats</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Loud fast</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">Urgent, strong knee pats</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no artistic reason"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about tempo?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nFast music is always happy and loud music is always angry, so each speed and volume has one fixed mood. That fails because the same fast tempo can feel playful in a game or nervous in a chase, and a loud sound can feel proud, warning, or simply clear. A mood word without the tempo or dynamic that supports it is a guess. Match the word to the choice you actually performed. Here is a more helpful example: Pat your knees four times at a slow tempo and a soft dynamic, as if you are crossing a dark room. Pat the same four knee sounds again, now fast and loud, as if you need a friend at the other end of a yard. The rhythm did not change: four single pats. Tempo changed from slow to fast, and dynamics changed from soft to loud. The first version can feel careful. The second can feel urgent. Those moods are effects of the choices, not new notes. Write \"tempo\" and \"dynamics\" on two lines so you do not blend speed into volume.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against choice weakens the work, then write a stronger choice with a reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nA strong answer separates speed from volume and ties each half to a mood with a because. The clicks stay soft while the tempo quickens, so the mood can tighten or flutter without becoming a shout. The stamps are both slower and louder, so the mood can turn heavy or final. Saying only \"it got more exciting\" never names the choice that caused the change.","explain":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the decoration without changing the artistic idea does not fix the error. Fast music is always happy and loud music is always angry, so each speed and volume has one fixed mood. That fails because the same fast tempo can feel playful in a game or nervous in a chase, and a loud sound can feel proud, warning, or simply clear. A mood word without the tempo or dynamic that supports it is a guess. Match the word to the choice you actually performed. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no artistic reason","Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about tempo?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nTempo is the speed of the beat, from slow to fast, and dynamics are the loudness, from soft to loud. They are expressive choices: the same body pattern can feel cautious, urgent, heavy, or gentle when you change only speed or only volume. A choice needs a reason. You name the tempo, name the dynamics, and say what mood that pair suggests to you in this example. Fast is not locked to happy, and loud is not locked to angry. The mood comes from the combination and from the picture you are making. You can perform every example with pats, stamps, or a hum, then write the reason in pencil. Now choose a different subject or piece to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nMake or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your main choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should use the same arts idea as the model, but with different content or material.","explain":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new artwork or performance. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Transfer to a new artwork or performance"}],"title":"Year 7, Lesson 4: Aural skills and listening","unit_number":1,"unit_title":"Aural skills and listening","week":3,"week_in_unit":2,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nPat your knees four times at a slow tempo and a soft dynamic, as if you are crossing a dark room. Pat the same four knee sounds again, now fast and loud, as if you need a friend at the other end of a yard. The rhythm did not change: four single pats. Tempo changed from slow to fast, and dynamics changed from soft to loud. The first version can feel careful. The second can feel urgent. Those moods are effects of the choices, not new notes. Write \"tempo\" and \"dynamics\" on two lines so you do not blend speed into volume.\n\nWrite what makes this example effective, naming the element, technique or performance choice and its effect.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Two boxes separate tempo choices from dynamics choices.","caption":"Speed and loudness are different expressive choices.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Tempo</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">Slow beat or fast beat</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Dynamics</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">Soft sound or loud sound</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Two boxes contrast a soft slow pattern with a loud fast pattern.","caption":"The same pats change mood when tempo and dynamics change.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Soft slow</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">Careful, quiet knee pats</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Loud fast</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">Urgent, strong knee pats</text></g></svg>"}],"year_level":"Year 7"}') WHERE seed_key='s5-music-y9-w02-l2' AND family_id IS NULL;
